1
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:09,780
Lakshadweep. Just hearing the name, it feels like there must be a lakh of islands, right?

2
00:00:09,880 --> 00:00:11,180
But the truth is different.

3
00:00:11,260 --> 00:00:13,840
There are a total of 36 islands here.

4
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:16,150
Out of which only 10 are inhabited by people.

5
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:23,470
Another surprise is that the combined land area of all these islands is only about 32 square kilometres.

6
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:27,070
That means it is not even one-twentieth the size of Hyderabad city.

7
00:00:27,140 --> 00:00:32,060
But the sea around it? Almost four lakh square kilometres.

8
00:00:32,090 --> 00:00:35,120
Meaning the land is little, the sea is vast.

9
00:00:35,190 --> 00:00:39,260
That is why the story of this place is not about the land... but about the sea.

10
00:00:39,330 --> 00:00:42,640
In this video we will look for answers to three questions.

11
00:00:42,690 --> 00:00:44,380
How were these islands formed?

12
00:00:44,430 --> 00:00:47,720
How did people come here and how do they live?

13
00:00:47,750 --> 00:00:50,920
What challenges does such a beautiful place face?

14
00:00:51,010 --> 00:00:51,950
Let’s begin.

15
00:00:53,630 --> 00:00:54,910
First, one thing.

16
00:00:54,990 --> 00:00:59,430
The islands of Lakshadweep are not the kind of islands with rocks and hills that we usually imagine.

17
00:00:59,510 --> 00:01:01,650
These are islands made of coral.

18
00:01:01,710 --> 00:01:09,650
Millions of years ago, when the Indian subcontinent was moving northwards, a mountain range formed at the bottom of the sea.

19
00:01:09,690 --> 00:01:12,670
This is called the Chagos-Laccadive Ridge.

20
00:01:12,730 --> 00:01:16,910
The top of those mountains stayed just below sea level.

21
00:01:16,970 --> 00:01:21,320
There, tiny creatures called coral polyps began to grow.

22
00:01:21,350 --> 00:01:24,540
They are so small that they are almost invisible to the eye.

23
00:01:24,590 --> 00:01:30,170
But for lakhs of years, generation after generation, they kept building one on top of another.

24
00:01:30,210 --> 00:01:34,180
Over time the mountain below slowly sank.

25
00:01:34,210 --> 00:01:36,750
But the coral kept growing upwards.

26
00:01:36,830 --> 00:01:39,260
In the end what remained was a ring shape.

27
00:01:39,330 --> 00:01:42,530
A calm lagoon in the middle, thin land all around.

28
00:01:42,790 --> 00:01:44,890
This structure is called an atoll.

29
00:01:44,930 --> 00:01:51,630
That means every step you take in Lakshadweep is on a house built by a living creature over millions of years.

30
00:01:51,720 --> 00:01:54,740
That is why the sand here is so white and soft.

31
00:01:56,400 --> 00:01:59,250
When did people first set foot on these islands?

32
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:01,410
There is no exact record for this.

33
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:06,910
That is why more than history, oral stories and beliefs are heard here.

34
00:02:06,940 --> 00:02:14,310
According to the commonly told narrative, sailors and fishermen from the Kerala coast came and settled here.

35
00:02:14,340 --> 00:02:19,590
Later Arab traders started travelling on this Arabian Sea route.

36
00:02:19,640 --> 00:02:24,820
Because these were like roadside stops on the sea route.

37
00:02:24,880 --> 00:02:32,150
According to the belief of the local people, in the seventh century a Sufi saint named Ubaidullah came to these islands.

38
00:02:32,210 --> 00:02:37,030
His tomb is still there on Androth Island and people respect it.

39
00:02:37,070 --> 00:02:40,240
It is said that Islam spread here because of him.

40
00:02:40,290 --> 00:02:44,460
Today almost 95% of the people on these islands are Muslims.

41
00:02:44,510 --> 00:02:47,090
What grows the most on these islands is coconut.

42
00:02:47,150 --> 00:02:53,280
Making ropes, that is coir, from the coconut husk and trading it has been going on for centuries.

43
00:02:53,350 --> 00:02:55,710
Land is less, resources are less.

44
00:02:55,770 --> 00:03:00,010
Therefore using every available thing is the way of life here.

45
00:03:01,710 --> 00:03:07,730
In the medieval period these islands were mainly under the influence of the rulers of the Kannur region in Kerala.

46
00:03:07,810 --> 00:03:12,680
History says that the Arakkal dynasty there controlled these islands.

47
00:03:12,730 --> 00:03:18,040
Sea trade was very important in that period and the islands were a part of that chain.

48
00:03:18,130 --> 00:03:21,780
In the beginning of the sixteenth century the Portuguese came.

49
00:03:21,830 --> 00:03:25,580
Their intention was to gain control over the pepper trade.

50
00:03:25,630 --> 00:03:31,790
Thinking these islands would be useful for that, they tried to build forts on some islands.

51
00:03:31,820 --> 00:03:33,810
But the locals did not stay quiet.

52
00:03:33,860 --> 00:03:41,650
According to the popular narrative, on Androth they mixed poison in the food of Portuguese soldiers and drove them away.

53
00:03:41,700 --> 00:03:44,200
It is difficult to say whether this is a story or history.

54
00:03:44,280 --> 00:03:50,520
But one thing is clear – the people of these small islands did not easily accept the domination of outsiders.

55
00:03:50,560 --> 00:03:54,110
Later some islands came under the rule of Tipu Sultan.

56
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:57,280
After his fall they came into the hands of the British.

57
00:03:57,340 --> 00:04:02,160
Thus under British rule these islands were part of the Madras Presidency.

58
00:04:03,900 --> 00:04:07,730
In 1947 the country got independence.

59
00:04:07,760 --> 00:04:09,380
But there was a problem.

60
00:04:09,480 --> 00:04:13,180
Lakshadweep was in one corner of the map, very far away.

61
00:04:13,260 --> 00:04:16,520
No one there cared much about these islands.

62
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:20,070
But in terms of sea routes they are very important.

63
00:04:20,120 --> 00:04:22,910
There is a widely circulated story here.

64
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:30,030
Learning that the Pakistan navy was coming towards these islands, the Indian government hurriedly sent a small team.

65
00:04:30,080 --> 00:04:32,920
They hoisted the Indian flag on the islands.

66
00:04:32,960 --> 00:04:37,040
It is said that a few hours later the Pakistan ship reached there.

67
00:04:37,090 --> 00:04:39,400
But by then the flag was already flying.

68
00:04:39,450 --> 00:04:41,420
Whatever the story, the result is the same.

69
00:04:41,510 --> 00:04:43,490
These islands became part of India.

70
00:04:43,490 --> 00:04:48,460
In 1956 they got the status of Union Territory.

71
00:04:48,510 --> 00:04:53,090
At first they were called the Laccadive, Minicoy and Amindivi Islands.

72
00:04:53,090 --> 00:04:57,960
In 1973 the name was officially changed: Lakshadweep.

73
00:04:57,990 --> 00:05:00,510
Meaning “a lakh of islands” in Sanskrit.

74
00:05:00,570 --> 00:05:04,230
In reality there are 36, but the name is a big dream.

75
00:05:05,910 --> 00:05:09,160
Alright. What is life like on these islands now?

76
00:05:09,230 --> 00:05:12,790
First one thing – the language spoken here is Malayalam.

77
00:05:12,850 --> 00:05:16,360
Only on Minicoy Island is there a language called “Mahl”.

78
00:05:16,470 --> 00:05:20,040
It is very close to the Maldivian language Dhivehi.

79
00:05:20,090 --> 00:05:22,680
There is an interesting tradition here.

80
00:05:22,770 --> 00:05:27,010
In many islands property and house pass from the mother’s side to the daughters.

81
00:05:27,050 --> 00:05:29,280
This is called the matrilineal system.

82
00:05:29,330 --> 00:05:32,990
A son getting married and going to his wife’s house is also common here.

83
00:05:33,030 --> 00:05:37,330
So what is the livelihood? Fish, mainly tuna.

84
00:05:37,570 --> 00:05:41,250
Here the fishermen catch fish by the pole-and-line method.

85
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:44,910
Without casting nets, they pull up each fish with a hook.

86
00:05:44,960 --> 00:05:48,370
The whole world appreciates this as a sustainable method.

87
00:05:48,400 --> 00:05:52,310
Taking only as much as needed, not emptying the sea.

88
00:05:52,360 --> 00:05:53,750
Food is also the same.

89
00:05:53,840 --> 00:05:55,690
Fish, coconut, rice.

90
00:05:55,760 --> 00:06:01,010
It feels like our coastal food, but the taste has even more of the sea’s flavour.

91
00:06:01,060 --> 00:06:03,800
During festivals there are traditional dances here.

92
00:06:03,860 --> 00:06:07,280
Kolkali, Parichakali, and on Minicoy the Lava dance.

93
00:06:07,420 --> 00:06:11,180
These are mostly connected with sea life and work songs.

94
00:06:12,860 --> 00:06:16,540
Now, such a beautiful place, is everything fine?

95
00:06:16,620 --> 00:06:17,270
No.

96
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:22,370
Lakshadweep is one of the places in the world closest to climate change.

97
00:06:22,460 --> 00:06:27,340
Because these islands are only a few metres above sea level.

98
00:06:27,400 --> 00:06:32,160
Even a little rise in sea level increases coastal erosion.

99
00:06:32,200 --> 00:06:34,870
Some shores are already shrinking.

100
00:06:34,900 --> 00:06:36,790
Along with this, coral bleaching.

101
00:06:36,980 --> 00:06:41,950
When the sea water gets too warm the coral loses its colour and turns white.

102
00:06:41,960 --> 00:06:46,990
In 2016, the El Niño year, this happened on a large scale.

103
00:06:47,040 --> 00:06:51,200
If the coral dies, the reef that protects these islands also becomes weak.

104
00:06:51,200 --> 00:06:55,850
In 2017 Cyclone Ockhi shook this region.

105
00:06:55,890 --> 00:07:00,640
For small islands and their fishermen such storms are a very big blow.

106
00:07:00,730 --> 00:07:02,330
Another challenge is connectivity.

107
00:07:02,570 --> 00:07:10,460
To come here there are only two ways: by plane, that is the single airport on Agatti Island, or by ship from Kochi.

108
00:07:10,690 --> 00:07:14,970
Internet too has depended on satellite all these days and was slow.

109
00:07:15,030 --> 00:07:20,260
Recently efforts have been made to improve it through a submarine optical fibre cable.

110
00:07:20,330 --> 00:07:21,980
Fresh water is also a problem.

111
00:07:22,030 --> 00:07:24,820
Sea all around, but drinking water is scarce.

112
00:07:24,870 --> 00:07:31,490
That is why experiments like a desalination plant that turns sea water into drinking water are happening in Kavaratti.

113
00:07:33,190 --> 00:07:37,980
To go to Lakshadweep everyone needs permission, that is an entry permit.

114
00:07:38,030 --> 00:07:42,340
Because the environment and culture here are very sensitive.

115
00:07:42,370 --> 00:07:46,660
The islands are small and the load they can bear is also small.

116
00:07:46,710 --> 00:07:49,850
Tourism here is both an opportunity and a danger.

117
00:07:49,930 --> 00:07:52,040
Jobs and income come.

118
00:07:52,090 --> 00:07:59,330
But if more people and more constructions come, both the coral here and the culture here come under pressure.

119
00:07:59,380 --> 00:08:04,200
The question of whether we want development or conservation is always heard here.

120
00:08:04,240 --> 00:08:07,140
In reality it is not a question of “one or the other”.

121
00:08:07,240 --> 00:08:10,730
How to balance both is the real question.

122
00:08:10,800 --> 00:08:12,430
Let’s go back to the beginning once.

123
00:08:12,480 --> 00:08:19,070
A tiny creature invisible to the eye patiently worked for lakhs of years and built an island.

124
00:08:19,120 --> 00:08:23,950
On those islands people take only as much as needed and live together with the sea.

125
00:08:23,980 --> 00:08:26,810
The lesson Lakshadweep teaches us is very simple.

126
00:08:26,860 --> 00:08:35,650
Even if it is small, how strongly a place can stand depends on how carefully we look after it.

127
00:08:35,710 --> 00:08:38,730
If you liked this video, share it with your friends.

128
00:08:38,790 --> 00:08:40,530
We’ll meet again with another story.
